WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:05.029
Viruses are essentially protein packages surrounding
genetic material.

00:00:05.029 --> 00:00:12.190
They can’t survive without a host, and it’s
debatable whether or not they are even alive.

00:00:12.190 --> 00:00:17.230
We don’t have time to get into that argument
here, but what we will do in this video is

00:00:17.230 --> 00:00:24.590
examine some of the SARS-CoV-2 proteins—the
virus responsible for the disease COVID-19.

00:00:24.590 --> 00:00:29.570
By now, you’ve probably seen many images
like this one—these artistic renderings

00:00:29.570 --> 00:00:31.330
of coronaviruses.

00:00:31.330 --> 00:00:36.830
Coronaviruses get their name from the spikes
that protrude from the surface of a viral

00:00:36.830 --> 00:00:41.510
envelope that sort of resemble a solar corona.

00:00:41.510 --> 00:00:46.420
These spikes are actually the proteins that
bind to human cells and allow the virus to

00:00:46.420 --> 00:00:48.120
infect them.

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:55.190
Amazingly, we already have a lot of information
about these spike or S-proteins.

00:00:55.190 --> 00:01:00.690
The proteins are similar to other members
of the coronaviridae family, so researchers

00:01:00.690 --> 00:01:05.960
were able to build on that knowledge and rather
quickly create the structures that we’ll

00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:07.219
learn about here.

00:01:07.219 --> 00:01:12.990
We’ll begin with a protein structure that
was solved by cryogenic electron microscopy,

00:01:12.990 --> 00:01:15.340
or Cryo EM.

00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:20.619
If we move from this view looking down at
the viral membrane, the spike protein begins

00:01:20.619 --> 00:01:26.970
to appear less triangular, and we can see
it has a roughly cylindrical shape.

00:01:26.970 --> 00:01:33.600
Notice that it is heavily glycosylated—during
viral replication, it uses our body’s own

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:41.270
enzymes to covalently attach sugars to asparagine
side chains near the protein surface.

00:01:41.270 --> 00:01:47.039
It’s thought that glycosylation plays a
role in protecting the virus against our own

00:01:47.039 --> 00:01:50.170
immune system.

00:01:50.170 --> 00:01:55.219
As we zoom back out, we’re now looking at
the ribbon diagram of the protein, and if

00:01:55.219 --> 00:02:01.659
we color the alpha helices pink and beta sheets
yellow, we see a section of alpha helices

00:02:01.659 --> 00:02:04.899
running the length of the spike protein.

00:02:04.899 --> 00:02:10.409
For the most part, the beta sheets are concentrated
on this end, which is where the spike protein

00:02:10.409 --> 00:02:13.570
fuses with a cell to infect it.

00:02:13.570 --> 00:02:19.470
The spike protein is actually made up of three
intertwined chains that have identical amino

00:02:19.470 --> 00:02:24.860
acid sequences—each of these chains is called
a protomer.

00:02:24.860 --> 00:02:30.730
However, the protomers do not have identical
three-dimensional conformations.

00:02:30.730 --> 00:02:34.319
Let’s go back to our original color scheme.

00:02:34.319 --> 00:02:38.939
We can see the conformational difference in
the protomers by examining a section of the

00:02:38.939 --> 00:02:45.629
spike protein that is critical to the viral
life cycle, the receptor binding domain or

00:02:45.629 --> 00:02:46.629
RBD.

00:02:46.629 --> 00:02:54.319
The RBD is where this virus binds to an enzyme
on the host cell’s surface, enabling it

00:02:54.319 --> 00:02:59.049
to fuse with the cell and transport the viral
genetic material inside.

00:02:59.049 --> 00:03:07.180
Two of these RBDs are in a down conformation
in this structure—let’s color these yellow.

00:03:07.180 --> 00:03:11.810
However, one of these RBDs is flipped up.

00:03:11.810 --> 00:03:17.450
This “up” conformation is higher energy,
poised to bind to the cellular receptor and

00:03:17.450 --> 00:03:19.310
result in fusion.

00:03:19.310 --> 00:03:25.609
It’s thought that when the spike protein
binds, each of these RBDs shifts into this

00:03:25.609 --> 00:03:29.099
less stable “up" conformation.

00:03:29.099 --> 00:03:36.080
Our own peptide-bond-breaking enzymes called
proteases can cut the spike protein at specific

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:42.969
sites, and conformational changes in the spike
protein enable fusion to occur.

00:03:42.969 --> 00:03:49.069
A different research group published a crystal
structure of just the receptor binding domain

00:03:49.069 --> 00:03:50.329
of the spike protein.

00:03:50.329 --> 00:03:53.639
We’ll keep the RBD colored white.

00:03:53.639 --> 00:04:01.459
In this structure, the RBD is bound to angiotensin-converting
enzyme 2—also called ACE2, which is the

00:04:01.459 --> 00:04:07.859
receptor on our cell surface that the coronavirus
binds to cause fusion.

00:04:07.859 --> 00:04:13.329
Notice these structures are heavily glycosylated
as well—let’s hide the sugars in this

00:04:13.329 --> 00:04:19.570
model, so we can focus in on some of the weak
binding interactions that hold the RBD and

00:04:19.570 --> 00:04:21.260
ACE2 together.

00:04:21.260 --> 00:04:28.070
For example, we have an extensive hydrogen
bonding network at the RBD-ACE2 interface

00:04:28.070 --> 00:04:32.110
involving these two tyrosine residues.

00:04:32.110 --> 00:04:37.200
This tyrosine side chain is also hydrogen
bonded to the carbonyl of this asparagine

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:44.320
side chain, which in turn bonds through its
N-H hydrogen atom to the carbonyl of this

00:04:44.320 --> 00:04:48.340
glutamine on ACE2.

00:04:48.340 --> 00:04:54.130
Moving along the ACE2 alpha helix, we have
a glutamate side chain which is deprotonated

00:04:54.130 --> 00:05:01.440
at pH 7.4, and a lysine residue that carries
a positive charge at that pH.

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:06.940
These residues are both involved in hydrogen
bonding with the amide of a glutamine side

00:05:06.940 --> 00:05:08.340
chain.

00:05:08.340 --> 00:05:13.880
Notice that these hydrogen bonds are relatively
short, owing to the strong hydrogen bonds

00:05:13.880 --> 00:05:19.860
that can form when charged amino acid side
chains are involved.

00:05:19.860 --> 00:05:26.260
In this region, we have extensive hydrogen
bonding—we see some between amino acid backbone

00:05:26.260 --> 00:05:32.550
atoms, and some additional hydrogen bonds
among polar and charged residues, like the

00:05:32.550 --> 00:05:38.950
hydrogen bonds of this tyrosine with a negatively
charged glutamate side chain and a positively

00:05:38.950 --> 00:05:41.580
charged arginine side chain.

00:05:41.580 --> 00:05:47.410
Overall, characterization of the spike protein
is super important because this paves the

00:05:47.410 --> 00:05:50.130
way for vaccine development.

00:05:50.130 --> 00:05:55.860
If we’re able to prime our immune system
by introducing small, harmless peptides that

00:05:55.860 --> 00:06:02.690
resemble the spike protein, our immune system
can recognize and build up antibodies against

00:06:02.690 --> 00:06:07.980
it, protecting us against COVID-19 infection
in the future.

00:06:07.980 --> 00:06:14.280
Also, knowing what the RBD binds to could
help us develop treatments that tie up the

00:06:14.280 --> 00:06:22.210
virus by binding it outside of the cell, before
it is able to fuse and cause infection.

00:06:22.210 --> 00:06:28.690
If the virus does undergo fusion, viral genetic
material is released into the cell.

00:06:28.690 --> 00:06:34.780
In the case of coronaviruses, this piece of
RNA travels to our own cell’s ribosomes

00:06:34.780 --> 00:06:38.710
and hijacks them to create its own viral proteins.

00:06:38.710 --> 00:06:43.960
An interesting thing I learned making this
video is that this viral RNA is capable of

00:06:43.960 --> 00:06:50.560
shifting the three-letter frame of RNA bases
that is read by the ribosome—this essentially

00:06:50.560 --> 00:06:57.330
doubles the peptide sequence that can be made
from one single strand of viral RNA.

00:06:57.330 --> 00:07:02.590
Viral translation using our ribosomes makes
the proteins that the virus needs to assemble

00:07:02.590 --> 00:07:08.260
additional copies of itself, which will eventually
be released from the cell and infect other

00:07:08.260 --> 00:07:09.260
cells.

00:07:09.260 --> 00:07:15.500
There’s an important protein that is translated
in this process, and that is the main protease

00:07:15.500 --> 00:07:21.160
that cleaves the viral polypeptide chain into
the functional proteins needed to assemble

00:07:21.160 --> 00:07:23.610
new viruses.

00:07:23.610 --> 00:07:29.460
This is another therapeutic target—if an
individual is already infected with the virus,

00:07:29.460 --> 00:07:35.760
but you can administer a drug that binds to
the protease and stops it from creating mature

00:07:35.760 --> 00:07:41.110
viral proteins, viral replication can be slowed.

00:07:41.110 --> 00:07:48.040
This main SARS-CoV-2 protease is a dimer made
up of two identical protein chains, and it

00:07:48.040 --> 00:07:52.690
must dimerize to become a functional protease.

00:07:52.690 --> 00:07:57.370
There are many amino acid interactions at
the dimer interface, but the researchers that

00:07:57.370 --> 00:08:02.931
published this crystal structure suggest that
the ionic interactions between the side chain

00:08:02.931 --> 00:08:08.710
of this arginine residue and this glutamate
drive the dimerization.

00:08:08.710 --> 00:08:14.330
This interaction is present on both sides
of the dimer.

00:08:14.330 --> 00:08:20.050
Moving over to the active site, the important
residues are this cysteine side chain and

00:08:20.050 --> 00:08:22.140
this histidine.

00:08:22.140 --> 00:08:28.740
This enzyme is a cysteine protease, so it
uses the nucleophilic cysteine to attack the

00:08:28.740 --> 00:08:32.089
amide bond of a peptide.

00:08:32.089 --> 00:08:38.440
In the mechanism, the histidine nitrogen grabs
the proton off of cysteine’s side chain,

00:08:38.440 --> 00:08:41.940
allowing it to attack the peptide bond.

00:08:41.940 --> 00:08:49.490
The peptide bond is cleaved, and then a water
molecule can come in, releasing cysteine so

00:08:49.490 --> 00:08:54.710
that the protease can cleave another polypeptide
chain.

00:08:54.710 --> 00:09:00.220
Enzymes containing nucleophilic catalytic
residues are excellent targets for irreversible

00:09:00.220 --> 00:09:02.520
inhibition.

00:09:02.520 --> 00:09:08.810
Because they contain a nucleophilic amino
acid side chain—cysteine in this case—inhibitors

00:09:08.810 --> 00:09:14.940
can be designed that bind to the enzyme with
a permanent covalent bond.

00:09:14.940 --> 00:09:21.640
Unlike reversible inhibitors that can pop
in and out of an active site, these irreversible—also

00:09:21.640 --> 00:09:27.850
called suicide—inhibitors permanently inactivate
the protein, preventing it from doing its

00:09:27.850 --> 00:09:31.770
job and creating more viral proteins.

00:09:31.770 --> 00:09:37.070
These researchers had previously designed
inhibitors for other coronavirus proteases.

00:09:37.070 --> 00:09:43.860
They were able bind to one of these inhibitors
to the active site of the SARS-CoV-2 protease,

00:09:43.860 --> 00:09:46.620
and that’s what we’re looking at here.

00:09:46.620 --> 00:09:52.730
Notice the serine is clearly involved in a
covalent bond with the ketone of the inhibitor.

00:09:52.730 --> 00:09:58.820
Now, this is a reversible reaction, so this
isn’t a suicide inhibitor itself, but I

00:09:58.820 --> 00:10:04.060
still think it’s pretty neat to see the
cysteine tied up in a covalent bond in this

00:10:04.060 --> 00:10:05.690
active site.

00:10:05.690 --> 00:10:10.840
Over here, this carbonyl of the inhibitor
is making hydrogen bonds with three backbone

00:10:10.840 --> 00:10:14.000
NH groups of the protein.

00:10:14.000 --> 00:10:23.340
The catalytic histidine of the protease is
also involved in a hydrogen bond.

00:10:23.340 --> 00:10:30.330
This ring is involved in an extensive hydrogen
bonding network involving both backbone atoms

00:10:30.330 --> 00:10:33.570
and side chains.

00:10:33.570 --> 00:10:39.300
Knowing the contacts an inhibitor makes with
an enzyme allows chemists and biologists to

00:10:39.300 --> 00:10:45.760
consider the interactions and potentially
design even better inhibitors.

00:10:45.760 --> 00:10:50.880
Because COVID-19 infection is particularly
harmful to the lungs, this research group

00:10:50.880 --> 00:10:56.620
administered the inhibitor directly into the
lungs of mice by inhalation.

00:10:56.620 --> 00:11:02.420
The mice did not suffer adverse effects from
this treatment, and so this could potentially

00:11:02.420 --> 00:11:07.050
lead to promising COVID-19 treatments in the
future.

00:11:07.050 --> 00:11:12.440
Every new piece of information we learn about
viruses leads us to a better understanding

00:11:12.440 --> 00:11:14.820
of disease and treatment.

00:11:14.820 --> 00:11:18.279
You might be wondering what you can do at
a time like this.

00:11:18.279 --> 00:11:22.600
First of all, if videos like this interest
you, keep at it!

00:11:22.600 --> 00:11:26.690
Work on your understanding of disease and
structure, so that you can be part of the

00:11:26.690 --> 00:11:28.930
cure in the future.

00:11:28.930 --> 00:11:35.070
Follow the hygiene and social distancing guidelines,
not only to prevent getting the disease, but

00:11:35.070 --> 00:11:40.670
to slow the spread of Covid-19 so we can keep
our researchers and the healthcare workers

00:11:40.670 --> 00:11:43.440
on the front lines safe.

00:11:43.440 --> 00:11:46.500
And be sure to take care of your own mental
health.

00:11:46.500 --> 00:11:51.120
Reach out to friends and family by phone,
text, and video chat.

00:11:51.120 --> 00:11:54.300
Spend time on your hobbies—get some exercise.

00:11:54.300 --> 00:11:59.321
We’re going to need that awesome brain of
yours in the future, so take good care of

00:11:59.321 --> 00:11:59.340
it.

